    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d451">

                <pb facs="0522" n="418" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg418"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/120: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-23T11:00:00+08:00">February 23, 1932—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 23—3:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>99. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 43, February 20, 5 p.m., and in
                    continuation of my 64, February 15, 4 p.m. Third report of League of Nations
                    Shanghai Committee was drafted February 20.<anchor n="88" xml:id="d451-f1"/>
                    Report covers arrangements for the meeting of the Chinese and Japanese military
                    on the 18th, unsatisfactory demands of the Japanese in regard to evacuation, the
                    delivery at 9 p.m. to the Mayor and commander of the 19th Route Army of written
                    Japanese demands (see my 82, February 19, 7 p.m.), summary of the replies by the
                    Mayor to the Japanese Consul General and by the Chinese commander to the
                    Japanese commander (see my 83, February 19, 10 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >p.m.</hi>]). The report states that no reply has been received by the
                    Japanese forces. Final paragraph of report is only important finding which
                    stated that “during the night 19th, 20th Japanese reenforcements were moved from
                    their base in International Settlement to the Japanese lines and after a
                    preliminary aerial reconnaissance which satisfied Japanese that Chinese had not
                    evacuated their lines in conformity with demands Japanese opened attack on the
                    20th at 7:30 a.m. in Kiangwan and Woosung areas.”</p>
                <pb facs="0523" n="419" xml:id="pg419"/>
                <p>It is important to note that the Japanese reenforcements were moved from bases in
                    International Settlement.</p>
                <p>I concur with the report.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation and Nanking for information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
